TI  - SAA-YOLO: A scale-aware attention enhanced YOLOv10 for UAV-based detection of medicinal plant Phlomoides rotata in alpine grasslands
PY  - 2026
T2  - Computers and Electronics in Agriculture
VL  - 245
C7  - 111570
DO  - 10.1016/j.compag.2026.111570
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105030158189&doi=10.1016%2Fj.compag.2026.111570&partnerID=40&md5=4e73dc9d9daeb1ccbd37bbe50cf7570d
AB  - Monitoring the population dynamics of medicinal plants is vital for sustainable agriculture and biodiversity conservation. However, traditional field surveys are constrained by labor intensity and limited spatial–temporal coverage. This study presents a UAV-based deep learning framework, SAA-YOLO, for spatiotemporal monitoring of Phlomoides rotata (PR), a rare alpine medicinal plant on the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau. The proposed model integrates a scale-aware attention (SAA) mechanism into YOLOv10s, combining multi-scale dilated convolution and channel-wise attention for scale-aware feature fusion to enhance small-object detection in complex alpine environments. Multi-temporal UAV imagery from 2020, 2022, and 2024 was used for model training and validation. SAA-YOLO achieved an F1 score of 96.89% and a mAP<inf>0.5:0.95</inf> of 80.49%, outperforming baseline and existing attention-enhanced detectors. Independent validation showed strong correlations with ground truth (R2 = 0.970 for abundance, R2 = 0.963 for canopy coverage), confirming high reliability for ecological assessment. Temporal analysis revealed a marked decline in PR populations, with individuals decreasing from 38,596 in 2020 to 25,168 in 2024, alongside a 44.0% reduction in canopy coverage. Although spatial clustering persisted, aggregation intensity weakened over time, indicating increasing vulnerability to environmental stress. Overall, SAA-YOLO demonstrates an effective, scalable solution for long-term monitoring of rare alpine plants. By generating high-resolution spatiotemporal datasets, this framework provides a decision-support tool for sustainable harvesting and conservation management of valuable medicinal resources in fragile alpine ecosystems. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
