AB  - The amount of gaps in forest canopy is related to the radiation interception for photosynthesis and visibility through the canopy. The dependence of forest canopy gap fraction determination on view zenith angle was calculated from polar-transformed sparse ((Formula presented.)) airborne laser scanning (ALS) point clouds for a Scots pine (Pinus sylvestris L.) stand growing on Kiriku Bog, Estonia. Visibility of ground targets was estimated from video image frames taken during drone (UAV) overpass at low altitude (40 m). Below-canopy digital hemispherical images (DHP) were taken in zenith direction as reference measurements. Angular grids of 3 (Formula presented.) and 5 (Formula presented.) were used to match the three data sources so as to decrease uncertainties in measurement geometries. The linear relationship between DHP and UAV data had (Formula presented.) = 0.67, with most of the deviations occurring at gap boundaries. Relationships over individual targets between DHP and polar-transformed ALS data had (Formula presented.). However, the simulation overestimated gap fraction at smaller zenith angles because of uncertainties in constructing lidar pulse footprints from point data. We conclude that regional coverage by means of sparse ALS point clouds shows potential for the assessment of forest canopy gaps at off-nadir angles. © 2025 The Author(s). Published by Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.
