AB  - The rewetting of drained peatlands to re-establish rare biodiversity of peat-forming vegetation is a long-term process. In order to assess this process, a monitoring concept is required that is operational, cost-effective and non-invasive to the sensitive ecosystem. Multisensory data collected by an Unmanned Aerial System (UAS) has great potential for such monitoring. The study area was a rewetted percolation peatland in Mecklenburg-Western Pomerania (Northern Germany). In an initial previous study it was shown that UAS data consisting of three different sensor types (RGB, multispectral, thermal), a digital elevation model and different vegetation indices are suitable to differentiate numerous, spectrally very similar, peatland plants. The aim of the current study was to test different acquisition dates over the vegetation period and to identify the most suitable period for classification. In addition, different acquisitions were combined into multitemporal and multisensory data sets to investigate whether different phenological courses of vegetation species contribute to the classification. All individual classifications showed very high overall accuracies between 84 % and 93 %. The best classification with 92.6 % accuracy was achieved in June, the high phase of vegetative vitality. Due to the multitemporal combination of four dates (beginning, 2x middle and end of the vegetation period) the overall accuracy was drastically improved again, to 98.6 %. Particularly weak classes could be consolidated by the phenological information. © 2019, VDE Verlag GmbH. All rights reserved.
